 "url": "https://www.martinfowler.com/articles/exploring-gen-ai/sdd-3-tools.html",
 "verdicts": {
  "Birgitta Böckeler (Thoughtworks) defines spec-driven development (SDD) as writing a spec before writing code with AI, with the spec becoming the source of truth for both human and AI.": 1,
  "Böckeler distinguishes three implementation levels of SDD: spec-first (spec written first and used for the task at hand), spec-anchored (spec kept after the task for evolution and maintenance), and spec-as-source (only the spec is edited by humans, never the code).": 1,
  "Böckeler found Kiro's workflow was a 'sledgehammer to crack a nut' for a small bug, producing 4 user stories with 16 acceptance criteria.": 1,
  "Böckeler found spec-kit overkill for a 3-5 point story, producing many repetitive, verbose markdown files; she thought plain AI-assisted coding would have been faster and given her more control.": 1,
  "Böckeler observed non-determinism when generating code repeatedly from the same Tessl spec, and found iterating to make the spec more specific increased repeatability.": 1,
  "Böckeler saw agents ignore spec-kit instructions, e.g. treating research notes describing existing classes as a new specification and regenerating them as duplicates, and also over-following constitution articles.": 1,
  "Böckeler's evaluation was anecdotal and limited (tools used in September 2025, small trials), and she notes no usage reports from long-term real codebases; she compares spec-as-source to model-driven development, warning of inflexibility plus non-determinism.": 1,
  "In Kiro, requirements are a list of user stories ('As a…' format) with acceptance criteria in GIVEN… WHEN… THEN… format; tasks trace back to requirement numbers and have UI elements to run tasks one by one and review changes per task.": 1,
  "Kiro's memory-bank equivalent is called 'steering'; its default generated files are product.md, structure.md and tech.md.": 1,
  "Kiro's workflow is Requirements → Design → Tasks, with each step represented by one markdown document inside Kiro's VS Code-based distribution.": 1,
  "Spec-kit creates a branch for every spec, suggesting a spec lives for the lifetime of a change request rather than a feature, so Böckeler considers it spec-first rather than spec-anchored.": 1,
  "Spec-kit is GitHub's SDD toolkit, distributed as a CLI that sets up workspaces for many coding assistants and is used via slash commands; its workflow is Constitution → Specify → Plan → Tasks.": 1,
  "Spec-kit's 'constitution' is a rules file of high-level 'immutable' principles applied to every change; the workflow uses bash scripts and templates to instantiate files, and checklists in the files track clarifications, constitution violations and research tasks, though AI interpretation gives no guarantee they are respected.": 1,
  "Tessl Framework (private beta) explicitly aspires to spec-anchored and spec-as-source, with a 1:1 mapping of spec to code file, generated code marked 'GENERATED FROM SPEC - DO NOT EDIT', and tags like @generate and @test.": 1
 }
}